<section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">EARTH OBSERVATION FIELD GUIDE</div><h1>HOW<br>SATELLITES<br>SEE.</h1><p class="hero-copy">Satellite images are measurements, not photographs. Learn how orbit, wavelength, resolution and processing transform invisible energy into information about a changing planet.</p></div><aside class="hero-aside"><div><span>OPTICAL</span><strong>Reflected sunlight</strong></div><div><span>THERMAL</span><strong>Emitted heat</strong></div><div><span>RADAR</span><strong>Returned microwave energy</strong></div><div><span>GIS</span><strong>Pixels connected to place</strong></div></aside></section>
<nav class="jump"><a href="#orbit">01 ORBITS</a><a href="#sensors">02 SENSORS</a><a href="#spectrum">03 SPECTRUM</a><a href="#resolution">04 RESOLUTION</a><a href="#pipeline">05 DATA PIPELINE</a><a href="#gis">06 GIS</a><a href="#reading">07 READ A SCENE</a></nav>

<section class="section" id="orbit"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">01 · ORBITS</div><h2>WHERE A SATELLITE FLIES CHANGES WHAT IT CAN SEE.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">Earth-observation satellites trade detail, coverage and revisit speed. Select an orbit to see how its behavior changes.</p></div><div class="control-row" id="orbitButtons"><button class="choice active" data-orbit="leo">LOW EARTH ORBIT</button><button class="choice" data-orbit="sun">SUN-SYNCHRONOUS</button><button class="choice" data-orbit="geo">GEOSTATIONARY</button></div><div class="orbit-lab"><div class="orbit-visual" id="orbitVisual" data-orbit="leo"><div class="earth" aria-label="Earth diagram"></div><div class="sat" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><div class="orbit-info"><div class="eyebrow" id="orbitTag">FAST · CLOSE · DETAILED</div><h3 id="orbitTitle">Low Earth orbit</h3><p id="orbitText">Most imaging satellites circle a few hundred kilometers above Earth. Being close gives them finer spatial detail, but each pass sees only part of the planet.</p><div class="facts"><div class="fact"><span>ALTITUDE</span><strong id="orbitAltitude">~400–900 km</strong></div><div class="fact"><span>BEST FOR</span><strong id="orbitUse">Detailed mapping</strong></div></div></div></div></section>

<section class="section" id="sensors"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">02 · SENSORS</div><h2>NOT EVERY SATELLITE TAKES A PICTURE.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">Sensors measure different kinds of energy. The same landscape can answer entirely different questions depending on the instrument.</p></div><div class="control-row" id="sensorButtons"><button class="choice active" data-sensor="optical">OPTICAL</button><button class="choice" data-sensor="thermal">THERMAL</button><button class="choice" data-sensor="radar">RADAR / SAR</button><button class="choice" data-sensor="lidar">LIDAR</button></div><div class="sensor-lab"><div class="sensor-info"><div class="eyebrow" id="sensorTag">PASSIVE SENSOR</div><h3 id="sensorTitle">Optical imagery</h3><p id="sensorText">Measures sunlight reflected from Earth in visible and infrared wavelengths. It can reveal vegetation, soil, water and built surfaces, but clouds block the view.</p><p><b id="sensorQuestion">Use it to ask:</b> What covers the land, and how is it changing?</p></div><div class="sensor-scene" id="sensorScene" data-sensor="optical"><div class="scanline"></div><div class="example-credit" id="sensorCredit">LANDSAT 8 · NATURAL COLOR · NASA / USGS</div></div></div></section>

<section class="section" id="spectrum"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">03 · ELECTROMAGNETIC SPECTRUM</div><h2>COLOR IS A CHOICE MADE FROM BANDS.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">A multispectral sensor records separate wavelength ranges. Analysts assign three bands to red, green and blue display channels to expose different materials.</p></div><div class="control-row" id="spectralButtons"><button class="choice active" data-view="true">TRUE COLOR</button><button class="choice" data-view="cir">COLOR INFRARED</button><button class="choice" data-view="swir">SWIR</button><button class="choice" data-view="ndvi">NDVI</button><button class="choice" data-view="thermal">THERMAL</button></div><div class="spectral-lab"><div class="spectral-scene" id="spectralScene" data-view="true"><div class="example-credit" id="spectralCredit">LANDSAT 8 · NATURAL COLOR · NASA / USGS</div></div><div class="spectral-caption"><div class="spectral-info"><div class="eyebrow" id="spectralBands">RED · GREEN · BLUE</div><h3 id="spectralTitle">True color</h3></div><p id="spectralText">Closest to human vision. Familiar and intuitive, but it hides much of the information recorded outside visible wavelengths.</p></div></div><div class="spectrum" aria-label="Simplified electromagnetic spectrum"><div class="band uv">UV</div><div class="band blue">BLUE</div><div class="band green">GREEN</div><div class="band red">RED</div><div class="band nir">NEAR INFRARED</div><div class="band swir">SWIR / THERMAL</div></div></section>

<section class="section" id="resolution"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">04 · RESOLUTION</div><h2>“HIGH RESOLUTION” CAN MEAN FOUR DIFFERENT THINGS.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">Every dataset makes tradeoffs. Finer pixels do not automatically make a dataset better for every question.</p></div><div class="resolution-grid"><article class="resolution-card"><div class="eyebrow">SPATIAL</div><h3>How much ground is one pixel?</h3><p>Smaller ground pixels reveal finer objects but create larger datasets and usually cover less area.</p><div class="range-label"><span>FINE</span><span>COARSE</span></div><input class="range" id="spatialRange" type="range" min="0" max="3" value="1" step="1"><div class="range-value" id="spatialValue">10 m</div><div class="pixel-demo" id="pixelDemo"></div></article><article class="resolution-card"><div class="eyebrow">TEMPORAL</div><h3>How often is the same place observed?</h3><p>Fast revisits help track fires, storms and crops; slower revisits may provide more detailed measurements.</p><div class="range-label"><span>FREQUENT</span><span>INFREQUENT</span></div><input class="range" id="temporalRange" type="range" min="0" max="3" value="1" step="1"><div class="range-value" id="temporalValue">5 days</div></article><article class="resolution-card"><div class="eyebrow">SPECTRAL</div><h3>How finely is wavelength divided?</h3><p>More and narrower bands can distinguish materials that look identical to human eyes.</p><div class="range-label"><span>FEW BANDS</span><span>MANY BANDS</span></div><input class="range" id="spectralRange" type="range" min="0" max="3" value="1" step="1"><div class="range-value" id="spectralValue">13 bands</div></article></div></section>

<section class="section" id="pipeline"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">05 · DATA PIPELINE</div><h2>FROM PHOTONS TO A MAP.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">The colorful image on screen is the end of a long chain. Select a stage to understand what changes.</p></div><div class="pipeline" id="pipelineSteps"><article class="pipe active" data-step="0"><b>LEVEL 0</b><h3>Raw signal</h3><p>Instrument measurements transmitted from orbit.</p></article><article class="pipe" data-step="1"><b>LEVEL 1</b><h3>Calibrated</h3><p>Corrected and located accurately on Earth.</p></article><article class="pipe" data-step="2"><b>LEVEL 2</b><h3>Surface data</h3><p>Atmosphere removed; physical quantities estimated.</p></article><article class="pipe" data-step="3"><b>ANALYSIS</b><h3>Derived index</h3><p>Bands combined into NDVI, temperature or change.</p></article><article class="pipe" data-step="4"><b>GIS</b><h3>Decision layer</h3><p>Raster results joined with boundaries and context.</p></article></div><div class="pipeline-detail" id="pipelineDetail">Raw telemetry contains detector readings, timing and spacecraft information. It is not yet a map and usually cannot be interpreted directly.</div></section>

<section class="section" id="gis"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">06 · GIS FOUNDATIONS</div><h2>SATELLITE DATA BECOMES USEFUL WHEN IT KNOWS WHERE IT IS.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">GIS connects imagery to coordinates, boundaries and other evidence. These four ideas appear everywhere in Earth observation.</p></div><div class="gis-grid"><article class="gis-item"><div class="eyebrow">RASTER</div><h3>A grid of measured cells</h3><p>Satellite data is usually raster data. Every pixel has a location and one or more numerical band values.</p><div class="mini-viz raster"></div></article><article class="gis-item"><div class="eyebrow">VECTOR</div><h3>Points, lines and polygons</h3><p>Roads, rivers, fire perimeters and administrative boundaries are commonly stored as vector geometry.</p><div class="mini-viz vector"></div></article><article class="gis-item"><div class="eyebrow">CRS</div><h3>A rule for locating coordinates</h3><p>A coordinate reference system explains how positions on a curved Earth are represented on a flat map.</p><div class="mini-viz crs"></div></article><article class="gis-item"><div class="eyebrow">GEOTIFF + COG</div><h3>Pixels plus geographic metadata</h3><p>A GeoTIFF stores raster values and location. A Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF lets software request only the pieces it needs.</p><div class="mini-viz cog"></div></article></div></section>

<section class="section" id="reading"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">07 · READ A SCENE</div><h2>INTERPRET FIRST. IDENTIFY SECOND.</h2></div><p class="section-lead">Experts rarely recognize a place instantly. They build an explanation from several independent observations.</p></div><div class="checklist"><div class="check"><div class="check-num">01</div><div><b>Check the metadata</b><span>Sensor, acquisition date, band combination, resolution, projection and cloud cover.</span></div></div><div class="check"><div class="check-num">02</div><div><b>Orient the scene</b><span>Find north, scale and major land-water boundaries before interpreting small features.</span></div></div><div class="check"><div class="check-num">03</div><div><b>Read tone and color</b><span>Ask what bright, dark or colored pixels mean in this specific band combination.</span></div></div><div class="check"><div class="check-num">04</div><div><b>Read texture</b><span>Smooth water, rough forest, regular cropland and dense urban surfaces behave differently.</span></div></div><div class="check"><div class="check-num">05</div><div><b>Read shape and pattern</b><span>Geometry often separates natural systems from roads, fields, mines and engineered coastlines.</span></div></div><div class="check"><div class="check-num">06</div><div><b>Compare through time</b><span>One image shows condition. Two well-matched dates can reveal process and change.</span></div></div></div></section>
